<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Finance"
    categorySlug="guides/finance"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">No Employer Match? No Problem</h2>
  <p>
    I spent my first three years freelancing ignoring retirement. No 401(k) match means no urgency, right?
    Wrong. Turns out self-employed people can actually save MORE for retirement than regular employees. Nobody told me.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's the thing that blew my mind: with a Solo 401(k), I can put away sixty-nine grand a year. Those are
    2024 limits, by the way. Regular W-2 employees cap at twenty-three thousand. But when you work for yourself,
    you wear both hats. Employee and boss. So you get to contribute in both roles.
  </p>
  <p>
    I'll break down what's out there and help you figure out which makes sense.
  </p>

  <!-- Why Prioritize Retirement -->
  <h2 id="why-prioritize">Why This Can't Wait</h2>
  <p>
    Nobody else is handling this for you. That's the scary part. No HR department auto-enrolling you.
    No company match landing in your account while you sleep.
  </p>

  <h3>The Waiting Tax Is Real</h3>
  <p>
    Compound interest genuinely doesn't give a damn about your reasons. Someone who starts at 25, throwing
    $500/month into index funds, winds up with like $1.4 million by retirement. Give or take. Assuming the
    market does its usual 7% thing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start at 35 instead? That same $500/month only gets you to maybe $650,000.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wait until 45? You're looking at somewhere around $280,000.
  </p>
  <p>
    Waiting a decade literally cuts your retirement money in half. Wish someone had explained this when
    I was 22 instead of now.
  </p>

  <h3>Today's Tax Break</h3>
  <p>
    Traditional retirement contributions shrink your taxable income. Dollar for dollar. My CPA nearly
    hugged me when I maxed out my Solo 401(k) last year.
  </p>
  <p>
    Drop twenty grand into a traditional account while you're in the 24% bracket? That's like $4,800
    you don't pay in taxes. Not eventually. This April.
  </p>
  <p>
    Had a monster year? Stuff the account full. Slow year? Put in less. Zero required.
  </p>

  <h3>You're On Your Own</h3>
  <p>
    W-2 workers get nudged into saving. Automatic enrollment. Payroll deductions. Matching contributions.
    Everything designed to make saving easy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Freelancers? We get nothing. Open the account yourself. Figure out limits yourself. Remember deadlines yourself.
  </p>
  <p>
    Annoying? Sure. But there's a trade-off: higher limits and you call all the shots.
  </p>

  <!-- Solo 401(k) -->
  <h2 id="solo-401k">The Solo 401(k) — My Go-To</h2>
  <p>
    Some call it Individual 401(k). Others say Self-Employed 401(k). Doesn't matter. Same animal.
    I like thinking of it as the "make the IRS cry" account.
  </p>

  <h3>How Much in 2024</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>As employee:</strong> $23,000. Add $7,500 more if you're 50+.</li>
    <li><strong>As employer:</strong> Up to a quarter of net self-employment earnings.</li>
    <li><strong>Put them together?</strong> Ceiling is $69,000. Goes up to $76,500 if you're old enough for catch-up.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Who Should Care</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Solo operators with no employees. Your spouse working with you is fine though.</li>
    <li>Anyone earning $50k+ who wants to shelter serious money.</li>
    <li>People who like having both traditional and Roth options in one place.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The Upside</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Biggest limits available.</strong> Nobody else even comes close.</li>
    <li><strong>Roth contributions.</strong> Some providers offer this. Game changer.</li>
    <li><strong>You can borrow from it.</strong> Please don't though. That's usually a bad sign.</li>
    <li><strong>Double dipping.</strong> Employee + employer contributions = flexibility.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The Catches</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Paperwork kicks in once you hit $250,000. Form 5500-SF every year.</li>
    <li>More complicated than a SEP IRA. Not brain surgery, but not instant either.</li>
    <li>Employee contributions must be done by December 31. Not your tax deadline. December 31. I almost missed this my
      first year.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Where I Opened Mine</h3>
  <p>
    Most big brokerages have free Solo 401(k) plans now.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fidelity</strong> works great. Free everything, solid fund selection.</li>
    <li><strong>Schwab</strong> has free accounts with Roth built in.</li>
    <li><strong>Vanguard</strong> is Vanguard. Boring in the best way.</li>
    <li><strong>E*TRADE</strong> if you're into trading. I'm not.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- SEP IRA -->
  <h2 id="sep-ira">SEP IRAs Made Simple</h2>
  <p>
    The Simplified Employee Pension. Less paperwork than a Solo 401(k). Still good limits.
  </p>

  <h3>2024 Numbers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cap:</strong> 25% of net self-employment income, maxing at $69,000.</li>
    <li>Only employer contributions exist here. No employee piece.</li>
    <li>No catch-up for the 50+ crowd. Bummer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Works For</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>People who want dead simple. Open it, fund it, done.</li>
    <li>Freelancers with employees. Warning: this gets pricey. You contribute the same percentage for everyone.</li>
    <li>Variable earners who like percentage-based math.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why It's Nice</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Takes five minutes to set up.</strong> Literally.</li>
    <li><strong>No annual forms.</strong> Zero. None.</li>
    <li><strong>Contribute anytime</strong> until tax day, including extensions. October 15 if you extend.</li>
    <li><strong>No minimums.</strong> Skip a year if things are tight.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Watch Out For</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Traditional only. No Roth flavor.</li>
    <li>Lower effective limits at lower incomes since it's percentage-based.</li>
    <li>Employees get the same deal you do. That adds up fast.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>SEP vs Solo 401(k) Head to Head</h3>
  <p>
    The math diverges at higher incomes because Solo 401(k)s have that employee piece. Making $100k net?
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>SEP maxes around $18,587.</strong> That's your 25%.</li>
    <li><strong>Solo 401(k) hits about $41,587.</strong> The $23,000 employee part plus employer match.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Not close.</p>

  <!-- SIMPLE IRA -->
  <h2 id="simple-ira">SIMPLE IRAs (If You Have Staff)</h2>
  <p>
    That stands for Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees. Real catchy name, government.
    Anyway, it's built for small teams.
  </p>

  <h3>2024 Limits</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Employee:</strong> $16,000. Add $3,500 if over 50.</li>
    <li><strong>Employer</strong> kicks in 3% match or 2% flat contribution.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Good Match For</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Teams of 1 to 100 people.</li>
    <li>Business owners who want simple benefit administration.</li>
    <li>Anyone trying to attract talent with retirement perks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Positives</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Your people can actually contribute. SEP doesn't allow that.</li>
    <li>Less admin headache than traditional 401(k) plans.</li>
    <li>Everyone vests immediately.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Downsides</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Smaller limits than SEP or Solo 401(k).</li>
    <li>Withdraw in the first two years? 25% penalty. Regular accounts charge 10%. Ouch.</li>
    <li>Can't run another retirement plan alongside it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Most solo freelancers should skip this. SEP or Solo 401(k) wins. SIMPLE makes sense when you've
    got employees who want in.
  </p>

  <!-- Traditional & Roth IRA -->
  <h2 id="ira">Old School IRAs</h2>
  <p>
    The backup singers. Small limits but great as supplements.
  </p>

  <h3>2024 Max</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>$7,000.</strong> Bump to $8,000 at 50+.</li>
    <li>Total across all IRAs, traditional plus Roth combined.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Traditional Flavor</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Put money in, get deduction. Pull it out later, pay taxes then.</li>
    <li>Fully deductible at any income if you have no workplace plan.</li>
    <li>Forced withdrawals start at 73. RMDs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Roth Flavor</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>No deduction today. Tax-free money forever in retirement.</li>
    <li>There are income limits though. Phase-out kicks in around $146k if you're single, $230k married.</li>
    <li>Can yank your contributions anytime. Just not the growth.</li>
    <li>Never forced to withdraw. Leave it to your kids, whatever.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The Backdoor Trick</h3>
  <p>
    Too rich for regular Roth? Back door's open.
  </p>
  <p>
    Put money in a non-deductible traditional IRA. Convert it to Roth. Pay tax only on gains,
    which are basically zero if you convert fast.
  </p>
  <p>
    Totally legal. Just document everything. Talk to a CPA if you've got old traditional IRA money
    sitting around. Pro-rata rules make things messy.
  </p>

