  <h3>Quick Guide</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Under $50k income:</strong> Start with a basic IRA. Traditional or Roth, your call.</li>
    <li><strong>$50k to $150k:</strong> SEP for lazy, Solo 401(k) for aggressive savers.</li>
    <li><strong>Over $150k:</strong> Solo 401(k) all day. Hit that $69k ceiling.</li>
    <li><strong>Got a team:</strong> SEP keeps it simple. SIMPLE IRA if employees want to chip in.</li>
    <li><strong>Want Roth:</strong> Solo 401(k) with Roth or separate Roth IRA.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- How to Open -->
  <h2 id="how-to-open">How to Actually Do This</h2>

  <h3>Setting Up a Solo 401(k)</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Go with Fidelity, Schwab, or Vanguard. All three work great.</li>
    <li>Fill out their plan adoption form. Maybe 20 minutes online.</li>
    <li>Enter your EIN. SSN works for sole proprietors.</li>
    <li>Fund it however. Transfer, check, doesn't matter.</li>
    <li>Pick some funds. Target date funds are fine to start.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Key date:</strong> plan must exist by December 31 of the tax year.
  </p>

  <h3>Opening a SEP IRA</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Find a brokerage with SEP IRA option.</li>
    <li>Complete IRS Form 5305-SEP. The provider handles this.</li>
    <li>Open the account.</li>
    <li>Send money.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Deadline:</strong> tax filing day including extensions.
  </p>

  <h3>Opening a Regular IRA</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Any brokerage. Seriously, they all have these.</li>
    <li>Ten minutes online.</li>
    <li>Connect your bank.</li>
    <li>Transfer cash.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Must be done by April 15. No extensions here.
  </p>

  <!-- Automation -->
  <h2 id="automation">Making It Automatic</h2>
  <p>
    The only retirement plan that works is one you'll actually follow. Automation beats willpower every time.
  </p>

  <h3>Percentage Approach</h3>
  <p>
    Pick a percentage. Whatever feels sustainable.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bare minimum?</strong> Probably 10-15% of gross.</li>
    <li><strong>Aggressive?</strong> 20-25% if you wanna retire before you're ancient.</li>
    <li>Move money the instant clients pay you. Before you spend any.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Auto Transfers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Monthly recurring transfers from checking to retirement.</li>
    <li>Income bounces around? Pick a safe base amount. Top up manually when you have surplus.</li>
    <li>Most brokerages let you automate this.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Pay Yourself First</h3>
  <p>
    Build your money flow so retirement gets paid before anything else.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Client pays invoice.</li>
    <li>Retirement transfer fires immediately.</li>
    <li>Taxes move to savings at 25-30%.</li>
    <li>What's left goes to operating and personal accounts.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Quarterly Check-In</h3>
  <p>
    Look at contributions every three months.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Add up year-to-date income.</li>
    <li>Guess full year.</li>
    <li>Adjust deposits so you max out without going over.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Just Start</h2>
  <p>
    I wasted years thinking I'd handle retirement "when I'm making real money." Worst financial decision of my life.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Open something this week. Fidelity or Schwab or Vanguard. All free to start.</li>
    <li>Pick an amount. Any amount. $100/month beats zero.</li>
    <li>Set it up automatic. Willpower fades. Automation doesn't.</li>
    <li>Bump it up as income grows.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The freedom of freelancing should build wealth, not leave you broke at 65. Future you is watching.
  </p>

